In the Flatpak a crash leaves a minidump (#2454). Without Flatpak's devel permission, its seccomp
refuses the ptrace syscall, which Crashpad's handler attaches with, so it wrote none. The kernel
still lets the handler read a process that named it its ptracer through /proc: its memory, and each
thread's stack pointer and program counter. When ptrace is refused, the handler stops the process
with SIGSTOP, as ptrace would hold it, reads it there, and lets it go on. The crashing thread's whole
context comes from the client, as it always has; the other threads have their stacks and where they
were, but no other registers. Renderers, which zypak starts in a pid namespace of their own, can't
name the handler, whose pid means nothing there, so in the Flatpak they allow any ptracer instead,
before their sandbox engages. Outside the Flatpak nothing changes.

